1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,040
So, hello. This past OKR, the Academy team, that's me and Adam, have been working on some

2
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:26,860
new HTTP2 labs based on James's new research on the topic. So the Web Security Academy

3
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:36,920
is basically a free online resource that you can use to learn about vulnerabilities that you can find in websites.

4
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:45,220
So you can bring up a small little sandbox website, which is vulnerable to one little thing, which you can learn to exploit

5
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:50,260
and learn how these vulnerabilities come about. So it's a really useful tool.

6
00:00:50,260 --> 00:01:02,320
And basically, we've created six new labs which are going to be added soon to be released to the Academy,

7
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:11,320
which are eagerly awaited in anticipation of James's Black Hat talk three months ago.

8
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:19,380
So to get an understanding of this, I'm going to first off talk about what HTTP is in general.

9
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:31,380
So when you make a request to a website, your browser sends a request over HTTP to the web server and it sends you a response.

10
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:37,380
So if you're looking at HTTP 1, I'm going to show you a quick example.

11
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:41,220
So let's imagine this is you in a bar.

12
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:50,380
You go up to the bar keeper and you send a request. So you want the website up there.

13
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:57,380
They process the request and then sends it back to you. That's fine. It works well.

14
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:07,380
So this bar, they've heard about HTTP 2. It's meant to be faster than HTTP 1, the predecessor.

15
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:18,440
So they want to implement it. The only problem is they don't really want to go and teach their bar keeps how to understand this new protocol.

16
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:27,500
So they go and hire a waiter or they have a front end which can understand this new protocol.

17
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:35,560
They can understand it and then translate it to HTTP 1 so the bartender can understand what's going on.

18
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:39,560
So now you can send an HTTP 2 request to the waiter.

19
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:44,620
The waiter will translate this to HTTP 1.

20
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:51,620
The bar keep will process your order, give that to the waiter and then the waiter forwards it back to you.

21
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:57,680
So it basically looks like the website supports HTTP 2.

22
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:06,680
The problem happens though when there's an opportunity for misunderstanding between the front end and the back end.

23
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:15,740
So let's say you notice that when translating from HTTP 2 to HTTP 1 something can go wrong.

24
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:20,740
So you decide to send a really messy request to the waiter.

25
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:24,800
Like you want lots of sides, lots of substitutions.

26
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:31,800
The waiter, he takes that request and tries to tell the bartender what you want.

27
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:36,800
The bartender, he gets a little bit confused by this request.

28
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:41,800
So he sees the first thing there, beer, gives that to the waiter and the waiter's like,

29
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:44,860
alright sure, and then gives that back to you.

30
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:52,860
So then this other patron of the bar comes along and makes a request to the waiter.

31
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:55,860
The waiter forwards that on to the bartender.

32
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:59,860
The bartender, he's a bit muddled at the moment.

33
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:06,860
So he just sees what's in front of them from your last request because he's still trying to deal with that.

34
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:13,860
So he just hands that to the waiter and the waiter's like, alright sure, and gives that to Elon.

35
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:18,920
Elon asked for beer unfortunately, so he's not very happy with what he got back.

36
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:26,920
So this is basically how HTTP vulnerabilities can come along.

37
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:43,920
You can essentially smuggle a request and confuse the backend in a way that they'll serve the wrong request to the wrong responses to the wrong people essentially.

38
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:51,920
So the Academy Labs originally, well, they only supported HTTP 1 previously.

39
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:56,860
HTTP 2, I'm not going to go into the details, but it's a very complicated,

40
00:04:56,920 --> 00:05:01,920
complicated protocol to implement or support rather.

41
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:14,920
So in order to support it, we looked at some third party support like Apache and Netty that we could use instead of implementing it ourselves.

42
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:28,920
The only problem with that is they're meant to be secure by default, meant to be, and they're not easily configurable in a way that we could emulate the vulnerabilities that we wanted to.

43
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:41,920
Thankfully, the pro team were kind enough to use burps HTTP 2 stack to create a little library for us that we could use to support the protocol.

44
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:54,920
And after we got that, and a lot of back and forth, trying to get the labs working, we've finally got the six labs ready, and they should be released relatively soon.

45
00:05:54,920 --> 00:06:04,920
So to give you a little taste, I'm going to play a clip of me solving one for labs.

46
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:17,920
In this website, there's an admin of the site that goes there, that tries to log in every so often, and we can confuse the website in a way that will send the response to their login to us.

47
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:33,920
So we can steal their login and then delete one of the users of the site.

